now, uh I realized that it's not just

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.640
support in, quote unquote, what people

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.040
think as tech support. Uh it's a lot

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.080
more than that. If you uh create a Venn

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.080
diagram of my role, uh a PyPI support

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.440
specialist entails uh working with dev.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.120
So, I would even argue that it's more

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.280
dev rel advocate, dev rel relations. If

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.400
you look at the other side of the Venn

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.160
diagram of my role,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.720
uh you can see it's also uh you know,

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.640
what kind of features can we offer, what

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.240
kind of other services can be offered

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.320
our users through PyPI. So, I would even

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.320
say it entails a bit of technical

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.040
product. So, uh a whole gamut of things.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.600
>> And can you tell us something about the

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.320
history of PyPI and what is PyPI in

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.960
>> So, PyPI stands for the Python Package

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.360
Index and it was formerly known as the

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.480
Cheese Shop. So, hence where the term

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.760
wheel file came from.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.320
Uh, the whole Cheese Shop thing came

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.600
from the Monty Python sketch.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.920
Uh, and Python Package Index was created

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.400
out of a need to better manage and

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.920
distribute Python packages. So, it was

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.320
officially born in 2003

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.560
and I believe the first packages were

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.520
hosted in 2005. So, PyPI has been around

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.360
for almost 20 years.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.560
>> And how many people do work on PyPI? Are

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.400
you the only person employed to work on

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.760
it or are there more people in your

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.560
>> Uh, so prior to me, we had lots of

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.560
generous volunteers

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.040
dedicating their time and expertise.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.760
Uh, but right now I'm part of the Python

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.600
Software Foundation. So, I am

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.760
a part of the PyPI support team along

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.000
with Mike Fiedler who is a PyPI support

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.000
engineer and we also have our infra

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.760
folks supporting PyPI on the infra side.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.080
Uh, side. Uh, E Durban, director of

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.840
infrastructure and Jacob Coffee,

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.760
infrastructure engineer. Yeah. So, it's

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.320
four of us.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.800
>> What are some most common issues that

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.000
you're dealing with?

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.440
>> So, uh, when I came on, we had a huge

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:36.000
huge backlog of account recovery issues

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.440
which entailed, you know, people not

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.959
signing up for 2FA or people losing

00:03:40.959 --> 00:03:43.160
access to the recovery codes or 2FA.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.760
>> is not mandatory, right?

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.600
>> 2FA is now 100% mandatory.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.160
Uh, so if you're a PyPI a and you've not

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.920
signed up for Twilio yet, please do.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:04:00.400
Uh we also had a huge backlog of PEP 541

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.800
So, when I talk about backlog, the

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.840
backlog was delayed account recovery

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:08.280
specifically by 5 months and PEP 541

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.600
requests by a year and 4 months. But,

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.560
I'm happy to say that as of now, well,

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:14.959
with the exception of this week since

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:17.359
I'm at EuroPython, uh the backlog for

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:19.880
both account recovery and PEP 541

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.000
requests are current with an average

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.520
triage time for account recovery taking

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:28.000
between 1 and 1/2 to 2 days versus PEP

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.440
541 requests taking an average of 1 week

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:32.200
versus what it was before, which was a

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.320
year and 4 months.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.120
>> Yeah. And how many tickets do you have

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.800
in your backlog just to have some sort

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.000
of idea?

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.120
>> When you said that it was delayed, how

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.440
much of a work do you actually have?

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:45.880
>> Oh, that's a great question.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:48.120
Uh if I have to go back and look at my

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.280
my GitHub history,

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.720
uh I would say

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.200
over 500 plus that I probably probably

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:58.840
probably close. It's probably even

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.800
bigger than that, but just to just to be

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.240
on the conservative side, yeah.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.280
>> And are those some very easy tasks that

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.480
you can resolve immediately on your own

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:10.400
or does it require lots of coordination,

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.600
for example, with other teams or other

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:13.600
team members?

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.720
>> Uh that's a great question. It actually

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:19.040
depends on a lot of variables. It

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:21.160
depends on, you know, how fast the user

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.520
gets back to us because uh we do have a

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.280
set of processes, which I'm in the

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:29.120
process of documenting and creating a

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.200
uh to share with our users to for so

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.760
that they know exactly what happens when

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.520
they put in an issue, a ticket. Uh so,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.240
it really does depend on the user. If a

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:40.680
user gets back to us with the

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.880
information we requested from them,

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.000
uh you know, closing the issue is fast

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.840
versus if the user has any other kind of

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.840
um issues or lack of information.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.640
Say we require our users to have a valid

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:58.200
public repo of their project. So lots of

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.920
times users may not have that for

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.240
whatever reason. So those issues take a

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.280
little bit more time in terms of account

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:07.640
For PEP 541 the process takes a little

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:10.120
bit longer because

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:12.280
you know, I see you to summarize what

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:17.080
this PEP 541 is. Yeah, sure. So a very

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.880
version of a PEP 541 is say you have a

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:23.800
project called snake rabbit and

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.400
you created it. You had lots of hopes

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.240
and dreams for it, but then you know

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.720
life happened and then you kind of

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:33.160
stepped away and then say I come in and

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.560
I really want to name my project snake

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.000
rabbit, but you already own that space.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.280
You already own the name and the

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.400
project. So again, depending on the

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.800
variable if I want to continue to

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.360
maintain your project there's a

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.840
different process versus if I want to

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.040
completely replace your project, there's

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.400
somewhat similar but also different

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.760
process. So again, it's a nutshell a

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.480
TLDR version of what a PEP 541 request

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:56.840
looks like.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:59.960
>> And what if you ask user for additional

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:01.840
information but you're unable to reach

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:04.080
out to them for any reason? What

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:05.960
happens? Do you just automatically close

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:08.280
the issue after some time or what are

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:09.080
the processes?

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.960
>> I think that's also very context and

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:14.320
issue dependent.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.720
I make sure to over communicate with our

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:20.560
users to make sure that no matter where

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.680
in the world they are, they're hearing

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.760
from us

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:28.080
you know, we completely close the issue

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.760
due to you know, non response or things

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.280
like that. Account recovery, it's a

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:35.040
little easier to do because you know, if

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.560
a user's no longer interested in getting

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.160
their account back for whatever reason,

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.600
okay, they can always open a new ticket.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:43.640
For PEP 541 requests is a little bit

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:46.320
more complicated because the timeline as

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:49.840
stated in the PEP itself and

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.080
listeners or viewers to this can

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.280
Google PEP 541 and the PEP will come up

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.280
for some more detailed reading, but it

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:58.920
it really depends on the context of the

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.360
project and the communication

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:03.680
because there are times where

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.480
you know, users you know, there's a use

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.920
case where users have sadly passed away.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:10.480
But there multiple people who probably

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.160
want to you know, maintain the project

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.040
or replace the project with something.

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:17.120
So those things

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.120
take different kinds of courses of

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:22.200
processes and time then say two people

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.640
who are amicable about oh, you can

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:27.280
totally take over my project. So it's

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:29.080
really context dependent.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:30.400
>> Right. And you mentioned opening a

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:32.320
ticket. How do you communicate with your

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.680
users? Do you have a GitHub repository

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.719
where they create a ticket or do you

00:08:36.719 --> 00:08:39.320
have any sort of portal for users?

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:42.640
>> We we have our own GitHub issue tracker

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.520
and for

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:47.560
confidential stuff, we also after say

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:50.280
user opens a GitHub ticket for account

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.680
recovery or PEP 541

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:54.160
for any kind of sensitive information

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:57.000
such as email or any other thing, we

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.280
then our process includes

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:02.800
you know, using our own in-house email

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.080
kind of internal email system that users

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:07.080
can email us with more information and

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.560
the process goes from there.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:10.680
>> And do you have any self-service there

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:12.400
where users for example, they have any

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:14.200
issues that where they can go through

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:15.880
some workflow on their own without

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.040
having to communicate with you?

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:21.280
>> I think it depends if

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:24.800
they already say for example, they have

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:27.280
to a face set up, their account is you

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:28.760
know, you know, they still have the

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:30.280
recovery codes but they just need a

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:32.320
password reset. I think that is pretty

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.440
self-serving, but if for whatever

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:37.720
reason, you know, they did not sign up

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:40.920
for 2FA, then our systems automatically

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:43.040
unverified their email. And if your

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.680
email is unverified, then you can't

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:48.200
really do a self-serving password reset.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:49.960
So, just as an example. So, those are

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.320
some things that then a human being has

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.920
to come in and do all the checks and

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.280
make sure, you know,

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:58.520
help them out. Yeah.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:00.240
>> And you mentioned that most issues that

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.120
now you're dealing with are related to

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.520
account recovery and to an 2FA and PEP

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:08.400
501. 501. 501. [laughter]

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.440
Are there any other issues that you're

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.600
also dealing with or are there any other

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:16.320
new PEPs or new changes where you expect

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:19.040
lots of issues incoming in the following

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:20.240
weeks or months?

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:23.400
>> Oh, that's a good question. I think

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:25.720
support request that I've been tackling

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:27.680
are file and project limit requests. We

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:30.320
get requests to increase

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:32.440
the limit that folks can upload their

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:34.720
files or projects. Regarding future

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:38.200
PEPs, I'm not aware of anything new that

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:40.960
is coming up that might affect, but

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.920
yeah, so I would say for now, I don't

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:44.920
think so.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:49.640
>> One of new features is PyPI

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:51.520
What are they and how do they work?

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:54.560
>> Yes, so PyPI orgs was actually

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:57.880
introduced in 2023, but it went in a bit

00:10:57.880 --> 00:10:59.880
of a hibernation.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:02.800
But now it's back up again and it was

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.080
created to help larger communities or

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:08.520
larger companies with big projects with

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:11.400
multiple team members manage roles and

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:15.240
manage projects better.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:18.000
we're looking into increasing some

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:19.720
features for people who are signed up

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:22.520
with PyPI orgs because we want to know

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:24.600
from the community

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:26.760
what else can we do to make PyPI orgs

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:29.200
exciting because right now the the big

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:31.360
feature is, you know, if you're part of

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:34.280
a big community and you have several

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:36.160
people who need access to the same

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:38.280
project, you don't have to individually

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.480
worry about, you know, somebody leaving,

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:43.080
you know, uh, the project or package and

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:44.600
and everybody scrambling to get access

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:45.920
to it. Now, it's you know, if you're

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:48.880
part of PyPI orgs community or company,

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:50.480
uh, you know, it's it's seamless in

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:52.040
terms of role and project management.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:53.320
>> Right. So, that means that you have an

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:55.600
organization inside that organization,

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:58.440
you have some users or some groups

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:00.600
and admins and they can manage access

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:02.080
and everything on their own without

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:03.840
having to contact you.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:08.560
>> Uh, I mean, yes, unless they have a

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:10.240
>> And what are some existing features

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:12.240
there except of just having this

